130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Step 1: macrophages are attracted by the foreign agent/antigen such as bacteria and move toward it with the help of soluble substances such as chemokines and cytokines. This process is called chemotaxis)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Step 2: macrophages attach to the antigen \(infectious agent\) through their cell membrane receptors)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Step 3: Attachment induces membrane protrusions, called pseudopodia, to extend around the attached material)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Fusion of the pseudopodia encloses the material within a membrane-bounded structure called a phagosome, which then enters the endocytic \(internal killing\) processing pathway)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Step 4: In this pathway, a phagosome moves toward the cell interior, where it fuses with a lysosome to form a phagolysosome. The lysozyme and other hydrolytic enzymes digest the ingested material)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 50 0 R
/E (Step 5: The digested contents of the phagolysosome are then eliminated in a process called exocytosis)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
